# Commenting guidelines

- When code is unintuitive, hacky, or hard to read, add a comment that explains
  both what you’re doing and why. Examples are also very helpful.
- When you add a **larger new function** (roughly more than ~20 lines) or logic
  that is easy to misread, add a JSDoc (or focused block comment) that
  summarizes behavior, **why** it exists, and **concrete examples**—especially
  inputs/outputs or representative error messages. Prefer “what would go wrong”
  and “what does this return” over restating the implementation line by line.
  See [Code tells you how, comments tell you why](https://blog.codinghorror.com/code-tells-you-how-comments-tell-you-why/).
- Don’t write comments that only describe what the code does; write ones that answer “why” and likely reader questions.
- If logic came from docs/StackOverflow/blog posts/PRs, add a brief source comment so future readers can follow up on intent.
- Use JSDoc (`/** ... */`) for API documentation that IDEs or documentation
  tools should consume. Use `//` for explanatory comments, including comments
  before declarations.
- For multi-line explanatory comments, use consecutive `//` lines rather than
  `/* ... */` blocks:

```ts
{
  // We only need this constant in one place right now because...
  // Keeping it local avoids expanding the module's public API.
}
```

- In React JSX, put multiline comments in an empty expression using
  consecutive `//` lines. `redaction/jsx-multiline-comment-style` enforces
  this:

```tsx
{
  // Explain why this JSX is structured this way.
  // Include relevant context or a source link when useful.
}
```

- Keep a one-line JSX comment inline as `{/* Explain the JSX here */}`.
- Add a JSDoc comment before each React component, long function, or exported
  variable, and add comments for non-obvious props.
- If a prop’s type/name is ambiguous, err on the side of adding a clearer comment.
- When disabling ESLint for a specific line, always include the reason and rule name:

```tsx
// Grayscale background, so dark-mode handling is intentionally skipped.
// eslint-disable-next-line no-restricted-syntax
const containerStyles = getContainerStyles(theme);
```

- Avoid file-wide ESLint disables; prefer `eslint-disable-line` / `eslint-disable-next-line` with explicit rule names.
